{
  "schema": "trial/1",
  "trial": "P-42",
  "question": "On a pull request's head: after a push, is every required check pending again; does a required check conclude `timed_out`, `neutral`, `skipped`, `cancelled` or `stale` and keep that conclusion until the content changes or a person runs it again; is every check run read with its exact name and its App's id, two workflow runs on one commit both staying, since `filter=latest` is per check suite; and can any App with `checks: write`, or any Actions job, post a check run under a required Lens's check name, or a result for any criterion of a contract, one the contract does not name among them, which then stays on that head?",
  "pass": "Written 2026-10-29T17:47:22Z, before the run (issue 388's condition): a step held to a timeout-minutes shorter than its job's runs past it. Recorded for each of three jobs on one canary pull request: the step's conclusion, the job's, its check run's and the run's, and whether a later step with if: always() runs. (a) no continue-on-error; (b) continue-on-error: true on the step; (c) continue-on-error: true on the job. Issue 388 builds rule (a) only if (a)'s job concludes failure; if cancelled, rule (b). Either is an answer; nothing fails but a run that cannot be read. Run on 2026-10-29: the Maintainer's login pushed the workflow to a canary branch and opened pull request 73 (writes not recorded here). A step stopped at its own timeout-minutes concludes failure, GitHub's annotation saying it timed out; with no continue-on-error the job, its check run and the run conclude failure, and an if: always() step after it runs. With continue-on-error on the step, the step, the job and its check run read success. With continue-on-error on the job, the job and its check run read failure: it changes only how the run's conclusion counts the job, which this run cannot show apart, its other job failing. So a step's own timeout is a failure the Kernel already reads, and issue 388's rule (a) holds.",
